  "slug": "responses-biomarkers-14ee",
  "summary": "Recent multiomic and single-cell studies nominate immune-associated biomarkers of response to checkpoint and cell-based immunotherapies across tumor types.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the dominant thread is single-cell and spatial profiling of the tumor immune microenvironment, used to define malignant and myeloid cell states that associate with immunotherapy outcome. In glioma, a 69-tumor atlas shows that IDH status shapes malignant cell states and immune architecture, while a 38-tumor PXA multiomics study links glioma progression to tumor-associated immune responses. Colorectal and liver metastasis studies converge on neutrophil, macrophage and dendritic cell programs: senescent-like BHLHE40+ neutrophils drive angiogenic niches, SPP1+ macrophages associate with neoadjuvant chemotherapy resistance, and RORC+ dendritic cells are enriched in malignant ascites. Anti-PD1 response biomarkers appear in melanoma GeoMx profiling and in a mouse TNBC neutrophil interferon program. Several items are mechanistic or preclinical, so the biomarker claims remain hypothesis-generating rather than validated.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-05"
}
